<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Kōya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded 819</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Kōya-san, Koyasan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Kōya is a large temple settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama-prefecture">Wakayama Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, and the headquarters of the Kōyasan sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shingon-buddhism">Shingon Buddhism</a>, whose head temple is Kongōbu-ji. It was first settled in 819 by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a>, on an 800-metre plain among eight peaks said to resemble a lotus. The original monastery has grown into the town of Kōya, with a university of religious studies and 120 sub-temples, many of which lodge pilgrims. Its Okunoin holds Kūkai's mausoleum within the largest graveyard in Japan, and the third shōgun Iemitsu built a tomb there for the Tokugawa family. In 2004 it became part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-koya.jpg" alt="Mount Kōya" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danjogaran_Koyasan12n3200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shingon, brought by the monk Kūkai, who founded the monastery of <b>Mount Kōya</b> in 819, worked through symbols, rituals, incantations and mandalas.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
